# Keep line endings LF in the working tree, not just in the repo.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true on Windows rewrites files to CRLF on checkout, and the
# formatters (oxfmt and rustfmt both emit LF) then fail `pnpm fmt:check` / `cargo fmt --check`
# on files nobody edited. A single `git checkout -- package.json` was enough to break
# `pnpm verify` locally while CI, which checks out on Linux, stayed green.
#
# The generated app icons are committed (`pnpm icons` writes six PNGs and two `.ico` files).
# `text=auto` would handle them - git leaves anything with a NUL in the first 8000 bytes alone,
# and every PNG has one in its signature - but that is a heuristic, and its failure mode is an
# `.ico` with every 0x0a rewritten to 0x0d0a: a file that still looks plausible in `git status`
# and decodes nowhere. So the two formats are named explicitly. `binary` is git's shorthand for
# `-text -diff`.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.ico binary
